    "correct": false,
    "answer": "Stopped before finishing (the API stayed rate-limited through every retry). Best-effort answer based on what was found:\n\nsearch('Cambodia illegal wildlife trade report demand primarily fueling it country demand'): Report reveals Cambodia\u2019s appetite for tigers, rhinos and other endangered wildlife (0.789); Bengal tigers in Cambodia? Reintroduction plan raises questions (0.582)\n---\nread(https://news.mongabay.com/2026/07/report-reveals-cambodias-appetite-for-tigers-rhinos-and-other-endangered-wildlife): - *Illegal wildlife trade, primarily catering to Chinese nationals, is thriving in Cambodia, according to a new undercover investigation by Earth League International, a U.S.-based NGO fighting environmental crime.* - *Investigators identified five kingpins allegedly running transnational criminal networks in Cambodia that are involved in wildlife smuggling, human and drug trafficking and operate scam centers that defraud targets of money. ELI also mapped trade networks and gathered information on what species are sold and who buys them.* - *The report found many endangered animals and their parts, including tigers, elephants, rhinos and pangolins, openly sold mostly to high-ranking government officials, Chinese expats, wealthy businessmen and Chinese tourists.* - *The report urges China to act against Chinese scam center owners and corrupt officials who facilitate illegal activity and wildlife trade, along with reducing demand for endangered species within its borders.* Tiger bone wines in exquisite bottles. Tiger cubs sold alive as pets or served as a culinary delicacy alongside pangolin and cobra meat at exclusive restaurants. Rooms filled with rhino horns and elephant ivory, stacks of freshly skinned tiger pelts and bags of pangolin scales. Jewelry made from protected corals and ivory. This is just a glimpse of the thriving illegal cobra meat at exclusive restaurants. Rooms filled with rhino horns and elephant ivory, stacks of freshly skinned tiger pelts and bags of pangolin scales. Jewelry made from protected corals and ivory. This is just a glimpse of the thriving illegal wildlife trade in Cambodia discovered by undercover investigators from Earth League International (ELI), a U.S.-based NGO that probes environmental crime. From 2025-26, ELI investigators mapped out the people involved in this illicit enterprise and identified five main kingpins allegedly running it. They gathered details on how cartels smuggle wildlife and their parts, what they sell and who their main customers are. They also linked this black-market trade with human and drug trafficking networks and scam centers operating out of Cambodia that defraud their targets. The investigators\u2019 findings of this operation \u2014 named *Sandokan* after a fictional 19th-century pirate from Southeast Asia \u2014 are published in a new report. The yearlong investigation revealed a huge demand for wildlife products within Cambodia, fueled by wealthy Chinese and Cambodian buyers. \u201cIt\u2019s a trafficking enterprise, and a good example of what happens when you don\u2019t do anything \u2026 they grow and grow and grow, and at the end, the damage is substantial,\u201d said Andrea fueled by wealthy Chinese and Cambodian buyers. \u201cIt\u2019s a trafficking enterprise, and a good example of what happens when you don\u2019t do anything \u2026 they grow and grow and grow, and at the end, the damage is substantial,\u201d said Andrea Crosta, executive director of ELI. \u201cThe big surprise was the incredible quantity of wildlife in the hands of these people.\u201d Cambodia is a recognized hub of the illegal wildlife trade, with most products destined for China. But little is known about the scale of the domestic trade and, before now, the cartel bosses who allegedly run these criminal networks. \u201cWhat very often you don\u2019t know is who are the biggest players \u2014 who are the \u2018ghosts\u2019 who run the show,\u201d Crosta said. \u201cOur work is to give a name and a face to these \u2018ghosts\u2019 so they\u2019re not invisible anymore, because as long as they are invisible, they can do whatever they want.\u201d \u201cI think it\u2019s an excellent piece of work,\u201d said Debbie Banks. She serves as the tiger and wildlife crime campaign lead at the U.K.-based Environmental Investigation Agency (EIA), which also investigates wildlife crimes in Southeast Asia. Banks was not involved in this operation, but EIA has previously work,\u201d said Debbie Banks. She serves as the tiger and wildlife crime campaign lead at the U.K.-based Environmental Investigation Agency (EIA), which also investigates wildlife crimes in Southeast Asia. Banks was not involved in this operation, but EIA has previously uncovered illegal commerce in tiger parts in Laos and Vietnam in operations that also targeted Chinese nationals. \u201cThere\u2019s a lot of similarities in terms of how these networks are operating, and the role of the Chinese businesses and Chinese criminal enterprises,\u201d Banks said, comparing the current report\u2019s findings to her work. \u201cThat\u2019s interesting.\u201d \u201cThe investigation clearly shows that illegal wildlife trade is linked between Southeast Asian countries and China,\u201d said Russell Gray from the Global Initiative against Transnational Organized Crime, a Swiss organization that didn\u2019t participate in this investigation. He noted that the report reveals a much larger network and a global supply chain. \u201cThis is a wide-ranging international issue,\u201d he said. **A trafficking enterprise allegedly operated by Chinese kingpins** Investigators say they identified five cartel kingpins operating in Cambodia who allegedly run transnational criminal networks that smuggle wildlife from across the world. ELI reported that all are of Chinese descent and are well connected within Cambodia. Two of them Investigators say they identified five cartel kingpins operating in Cambodia who allegedly run transnational criminal networks that smuggle wildlife from across the world. ELI reported that all are of Chinese descent and are well connected within Cambodia. Two of them are believed to be the biggest wildlife traders in the lower Mekong region. The public report leaves them nameless; another version handed over to U.S. authorities identified all involved. \u201cWildlife trafficking in Southeast Asia is in the hands of Chinese nationals by 90%,\u201d Crosta said. He added that they have strong business ties in the region, with investments in real estate and a mix of legal and illegal businesses that include restaurants and clubs as well as scam centers. They are \u201cvery, very prolific\u201d wildlife traffickers, Crosta said. His team found that the two they identified as the largest traffickers own at least nine shops in Cambodia. Investigators saw many endangered wildlife products sold openly in their shops as well as online, sometimes to customers in other countries. Other businesses include a factory that carves rhino horn and ivory and a brewery for making tiger bone wine \u2014 an expensive alcoholic beverage brewed with tiger bones that\u2019s often sold in as online, sometimes to customers in other countries. Other businesses include a factory that carves rhino horn and ivory and a brewery for making tiger bone wine \u2014 an expensive alcoholic beverage brewed with tiger bones that\u2019s often sold in ornate tiger-shaped bottles. Though it\u2019s been used as traditional Chinese medicine for millennia, it\u2019s now become a pricey prestige item. Serving or gifting tiger bone wine is a way to flaunt status or seal a deal. Investigators also identified middlemen and buyers, including restaurant owners, businessmen and traffickers. One individual said he raised lions, tigers and primates on his properties \u2014 and allegedly trafficked primates, birds and bear parts, according to the investigation. Another said he smuggled wildlife products and narcotics to the West. Yet another said his role was logistics, claiming he could help traffic anything because he had close connections to Cambodian customs officials as well as the country\u2019s political, military and business elites. Meanwhile, the kingpins reportedly said they could supply any species that customers wanted: tigers, elephants, rhinos, pangolins, sea turtles, coral, bears, primates and birds of prey \u2014 either alive, dead or in parts. They said they had suppliers across the globe, in Africa, Europe, [...article truncated...]",
